        "nl-extract.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "8854d258-a5b1-32bf-a507-c73705b90eb1",
                "word": "scrupule",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "nl",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "4e1fc725-6718-4974-ad01-14009dacb3ef",
                        "entry_id": "8854d258-a5b1-32bf-a507-c73705b90eb1",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "scrupules"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "0d3fb740-01bd-3abe-a015-6537a531d95d",
                        "entry_id": "8854d258-a5b1-32bf-a507-c73705b90eb1",
                        "glosses": [
                            "gewetensbezwaar, een reden om iets niet te doen in verband met ethische overwegingen"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "34bc472e-78f9-4a29-95f7-7a21ba15269c",
                                "sense_id": "0d3fb740-01bd-3abe-a015-6537a531d95d",
                                "text": "De sleutels van de Wetstraat 16 waren decennialang het meest begeerde kleinood in de Belgische politiek. Het premierschap stond synoniem voor macht, prestige en internationale zichtbaarheid. De Wetstraat 16 was alleen weggelegd voor wie een brandende ambitie had en zonder enige scrupule bereid was enkele broeder- en vadermoorden te plegen voor de hoogste functie in de nationale politiek. De vete tussen Leo Tindemans en Wilfried Martens is daar het meest sprekende voorbeeld van."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "0df08050-8e4b-404d-8090-a98e3650eeb4",
                                "sense_id": "0d3fb740-01bd-3abe-a015-6537a531d95d",
                                "text": "De Vereniging tegen de Kwakzalverij schrijft ontstelt te zijn dat Jan Smit nu wordt voorgedragen als voorzitter van de Raad van Commissarissen van de KNVB. ‘Zeven jaar lang accepteerde hij zonder scrupule forse geldbedragen afkomstig van zeer onzuivere bron. Iemand met zo’n kerfstok ontbeert ten enenmale het morele leiderschap dat ook in de voetballerij dringend gewenst is. In de RvC van de KNVB heeft hij niets te zoeken.’"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "a2d4b20a-2e7b-42fb-9840-d9097c2df617",
                        "source_entry_id": "8854d258-a5b1-32bf-a507-c73705b90eb1",
                        "source_sense_id": "0d3fb740-01bd-3abe-a015-6537a531d95d",
                        "source_sense_description": "gewetensbezwaar",
                        "target_lang_word": "scruple",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "scruple",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "0bec0388-15d8-38bf-9c04-a2cffe169ba4",
                                        "entry_id": "51d0d0a6-992c-38d3-9713-3c8e10ef1175",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Hesitation to act from the difficulty of determining what is right or expedient; doubt, hesitation or unwillingness due to motives of conscience; moral qualm."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "f17c28f0-d6ed-4b4d-9b2a-80fed3ecd435",
                                                "sense_id": "0bec0388-15d8-38bf-9c04-a2cffe169ba4",
                                                "text": "Before her flew Affliction, girt in ſtorms, / Gaſht all with guſhing wounds; and all the formes / Of bane, and miſerie, frowning in her face; / Whom Tyrannie, and Iniuſtice, had in Chace; / Grimme Perſecution, Pouertie, and Shame; / Detraction, Enuie, foule Mishap and lame; / Scruple of Conſcience; Feare, Deceipt, Deſpaire; / Slaunder, and Clamor, that rent all the Ayre; […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "5c72f033-ff71-4bb6-8bc0-2cbb73ee6cf4",
                                                "sense_id": "0bec0388-15d8-38bf-9c04-a2cffe169ba4",
                                                "text": "[U]ntil the Commonwealth [of England] torture was constantly used as an instrument of evidence in the investigation of offences, whether municipal or political, without scruple, and without question as to its legality."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "cabff2ce-f139-4f30-a6c6-ea9080463025",
                                                "sense_id": "0bec0388-15d8-38bf-9c04-a2cffe169ba4",
                                                "text": "The four chief sins of which he was guilty were dancing, ringing the bells of the parish church, playing at tipcat, and reading the History of Sir Bevis of Southampton. A Rector of the school of [William] Laud would have held such a young man up to the whole parish as a model. But [John] Bunyan's notions of good and evil had been learned in a very different school; and he was made miserable by the conflict between his tastes and his scruples."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "9c84354d-ea84-4696-999d-d20f48beda8b",
                                                "sense_id": "0bec0388-15d8-38bf-9c04-a2cffe169ba4",
                                                "text": "Ricky, I'm going to miss you. Apparently you're the only one in Casablanca with less scruples than I."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "154e307a-06bb-36f9-a2fc-07d7ecde9b2b",
                                        "entry_id": "51d0d0a6-992c-38d3-9713-3c8e10ef1175",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A weight of ¹⁄₂₈₈ of a pound, that is, twenty grains or one third of a dram, about 1.3 grams (symbol: ℈)."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "4af9d75c-b30b-4e95-a6c4-e95885fad69b",
                                                "sense_id": "154e307a-06bb-36f9-a2fc-07d7ecde9b2b",
                                                "text": "The Oyle ſerueth in many operations, and ſpecially in all colde diſeaſes, if they be inwardly, give thereof euerye morning one ſcruple to drinke, and if they be outward annoynt."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "eb38960f-3bc7-4c34-9106-d26d86560789",
                                                "sense_id": "154e307a-06bb-36f9-a2fc-07d7ecde9b2b",
                                                "text": "Bezoar (Horſe) called Hypolites, excites Sweat, reſiſts Poiſon, kills the Worms, and ſtops a Looſeneſs; the Doſe is from half a Scruple to two Scruples."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "9c21891f-c808-4bef-921a-3625d5a6bd3d",
                                                "sense_id": "154e307a-06bb-36f9-a2fc-07d7ecde9b2b",
                                                "text": "Marcus Empiricus, a Roma physician, prescribed three scruples of cress, three of red onion, three of pine seed, three of Indian nard, for impotence."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "9fcc827a-3ed0-3ede-bc4d-de08f7fc3944",
                                        "entry_id": "51d0d0a6-992c-38d3-9713-3c8e10ef1175",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A Hebrew unit of time equal to ¹⁄₁₀₈₀ hour."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "94253973-b3a9-4667-95ae-fb71e835c749",
                                                "sense_id": "9fcc827a-3ed0-3ede-bc4d-de08f7fc3944",
                                                "text": "If it be ask'd why the Jews divide the Hour into 1080 Scruples, the Author of the Neomeniæ ſet forth by Munſter gives this reaſon fo it, becauſe there is no number that is diviſible into ſo many ſorts as this of 1080 is; for it may be divided by 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 10, 12, 15, 20, &c."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "74cc05f3-0675-4766-96f2-ff8f2029ff71",
                                                "sense_id": "9fcc827a-3ed0-3ede-bc4d-de08f7fc3944",
                                                "text": "The Mahometan year consists of 12 lunar months, each containing 29 days, 12 hours and 792 scruples; (1080 scruples = 1 hour) so that the year contains 354 days, 8 hours and 864 scruples."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "75b9a885-a5cd-46e8-83fc-d540a2eaa3e0",
                                                "sense_id": "9fcc827a-3ed0-3ede-bc4d-de08f7fc3944",
                                                "text": "The most ancient hour was divided into 1,080 scruples. The Jews suppose Issachar to have brought it from heaven."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "611fae1c-ff87-3d0e-9983-30e3665def82",
                                        "entry_id": "51d0d0a6-992c-38d3-9713-3c8e10ef1175",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A very small quantity; a particle."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "broadly",
                                            "obsolete"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "384f9855-5a14-4c7a-80f7-f127c7c87b82",
                                                "sense_id": "611fae1c-ff87-3d0e-9983-30e3665def82",
                                                "text": "Why euery thing adheres togither, that no dramme of a ſcruple, no ſcruple of a ſcruple, no obſtacle, no incredulous or unſafe circumſtance: What can be ſaide? Nothing that can be, can come betweene me, and the full proſpect of my hopes."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "7d6cb442-edd9-3f3c-bdb1-9fed6523bfeb",
                                        "entry_id": "51d0d0a6-992c-38d3-9713-3c8e10ef1175",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A doubt or uncertainty concerning a matter of fact; intellectual perplexity."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "obsolete"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "93ab2418-dac7-4aec-922a-20e77266730a",
                                                "sense_id": "7d6cb442-edd9-3f3c-bdb1-9fed6523bfeb",
                                                "text": "There aroſe a Scruple, nay, it amount to a Queſtion, whether to attempt an Eſcape from my Patron, one that ſo dearly Loved me, ſo fairly bought me, were juſtifiable before God and Men?"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "scruple",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "c0785c52-a8aa-3d06-a80d-1ecf827c734f",
                                        "entry_id": "598ba760-fac5-3bad-8177-9555ff634e8d",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To hesitate or be reluctant to act due to considerations of conscience or expedience."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "intransitive"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "58be8027-d132-4476-8cf5-bb955b9b91b4",
                                                "sense_id": "c0785c52-a8aa-3d06-a80d-1ecf827c734f",
                                                "text": "They wouldn't scruple to cheat us if given the opportunity."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e6a92809-81cd-47ca-ae90-552f56048367",
                                                "sense_id": "c0785c52-a8aa-3d06-a80d-1ecf827c734f",
                                                "text": "On that day vvherein vve receive the Sacrament, vve are often over-precize, ſcrupling to ſay, or do, thoſe things vvhich lavvfully vve may. But vve, vvho are more then Curious that day, are not ſo much as Carefull the next. And too often, (vvhat ſhall I ſay) go on in ſinne, up to the Anckles, yea, our ſins 'go over our Heads."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ee5c3a63-6d53-4524-b1ba-7a29c0729e31",
                                                "sense_id": "c0785c52-a8aa-3d06-a80d-1ecf827c734f",
                                                "text": "But the Tenderneſs, we have to deal with, is quite of another Nature, being ſuch as one as makes Men ſcruple at the Lawfulneſs of a Set Form of Divine Worship, at the Uſe of ſome Solemn Rites and Ceremonies in the Service of God; but makes them not ſtick at all at Sacrilege, which St. Paul equates to Idolatry; […]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "2f75516b-1943-40ef-91c1-b254b9bd77b6",
                                                "sense_id": "c0785c52-a8aa-3d06-a80d-1ecf827c734f",
                                                "text": "We have the effrontery to teach Woman the doctrine that since we fail them both in quantity and quality, there is a life away from us and the children they could have from us that is worth living. We do not scruple to tell them that they can be happy, content, comfortable, without the surroundings to which they are primarily adapted."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c34fb0bb-9b38-46b6-ac01-a2b37dce62e1",
                                                "sense_id": "c0785c52-a8aa-3d06-a80d-1ecf827c734f",
                                                "text": "[...] it also charged the Newmarket Railway £600 a year for the management or rather—as the Chairman of the Newmarket Railway did not scruple to call it—the mismanagement of the line."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "bb556176-1538-3b61-bdf7-8cda233b3913",
                                        "entry_id": "598ba760-fac5-3bad-8177-9555ff634e8d",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To excite scruples in; to cause to scruple."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "intransitive"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "1e16921a-060a-4704-be74-04a45f1dfd66",
                                                "sense_id": "bb556176-1538-3b61-bdf7-8cda233b3913",
                                                "text": "It is granted indeed before that time, the Supream power was in Him [Charles I of England], and we were all his Subjects: and then perhaps ſome might Scruple to cut his throat, for there were lawes then in force against Regicides, but now ſince his Reſignation, (for ſo in our Tenents we hold this Act to be,) there is no ſcruple to be made, thoſe lawes against King-killers are ſuſpended, and he is now become as Samſon was without his ſtrength, […]"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "61fe58be-dca1-3f3b-b807-631c77ea8d7d",
                                        "entry_id": "598ba760-fac5-3bad-8177-9555ff634e8d",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To regard with suspicion; to question."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "transitive"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "06a51e01-2753-4e0a-8a18-24ababf9f2b4",
                                                "sense_id": "61fe58be-dca1-3f3b-b807-631c77ea8d7d",
                                                "text": "As for the writings of Heathen authors, unleſſe they were plaine invectives againſt Chriſtianity, as thoſe of Porphyrius and Proclus, they met with no interdict that can be cited, till about the year 400, in a Carthaginian Councel, wherein Biſhops themſelves were forbid to read the Books of Gentiles, but Hereſies they might read: while others long before them on the contrary ſcrupl'd more the Books of Hereticks, then of Gentiles."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c6b03600-9e15-4e19-95e6-5f84389e1d1c",
                                                "sense_id": "61fe58be-dca1-3f3b-b807-631c77ea8d7d",
                                                "text": "And notwithſtanding all the clamours of their inſulting brethren, they were ſatisfied that thoſe who were moſt forward for this declaration [that it was unlawful to take arms against the king], and moſt fierce in condemning thoſe who ſcrupled it, would not keep to it, if at any time they found things were come to extremity, as the event verified."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "de714239-5ae3-3d6c-abbc-4e98580db576",
                                        "entry_id": "598ba760-fac5-3bad-8177-9555ff634e8d",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "To question the truth of (a fact, etc.); to doubt; to hesitate to believe, to question."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "intransitive",
                                            "obsolete"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "77ef3f3e-8c60-4ce7-8ae5-76087a3bbb1b",
                                                "sense_id": "de714239-5ae3-3d6c-abbc-4e98580db576",
                                                "text": "I do not scruple to admit that all the Earth seeth but only half of the Moon."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
